course: biology
lesson: 211
title: Sheep and goats: wool, flocks, climbers and the tin-can myth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sheep and goats are cousins that people have kept for about ten thousand years, but they live very different lives.
explain: A sheep is a crowd animal: watch a flock at a gate and as soon as one sheep goes through, the rest pour after it, because for a prey animal the safest place is in the middle of the group.
explain: A goat is an explorer and a climber: its wild relatives live on cliffs, so a farm goat will happily hop onto a wall, a shed or even a parked car.
explain: Farm sheep grow wool that never stops growing, so every spring a shearer gives them a haircut, and a goat that seems to eat a tin can is really only nibbling the paper label to find out what it is.
fact: Farm sheep were bred to grow wool that keeps growing, while wild sheep shed their coats, so farm sheep must be shorn, their wool cut off, usually once a year in spring or early summer.
fact: A sheep called Chris was found in Australia in 2015 after years lost in the bush, carrying over forty kilograms of wool.
fact: Sheep stay together and follow each other because a flock is safer from predators, and they are not stupid: sheep can remember dozens of faces for years.
fact: A heavy sheep with a thick fleece can roll onto its back and be unable to get up; this is called being cast, and a cast sheep can die, so farmers help it up.
fact: Lambs skip and play, which builds muscles and friendships, and a ewe, a mother sheep, and her lamb find each other in a noisy field by their calls.
fact: Sheep nibble with a split top lip and can graze grass shorter than cows can; sheep, goats and cows all chew the cud, bringing food back up to chew it again.
fact: Goats prefer leaves, twigs and shrubs to grass, and in Morocco some goats climb argan trees to eat the fruit.
fact: Goats do not eat tin cans; they are curious and test things with their lips, so they nibble paper, labels and clothes.
fact: Goats have wide rectangular pupils that give a view almost all the way round, and when a goat lowers its head to eat, its eyes roll so the slit stays level with the ground.
fact: Mother goats and their kids, baby goats, know each other's voices, and a study reported that young goats' calls change to sound more like the group they live with, a little like an accent.
fact: Many goats have beards, both males and females; most sheep do not, and a goat's tail usually points up while a sheep's hangs down.
fact: Donkeys, llamas and sheepdogs are used to guard flocks, and a guard donkey will chase away foxes and stray dogs.
exercise: Why do farm sheep need a haircut every year? | Their wool keeps growing and never falls out by itself | Their wool makes them far too cold to go outside in the summer | The farmer needs to count their spots | Their wool turns into hair if it is left
exercise: What do wild sheep do with their old coats? | They shed them | They keep them forever | They eat them up | They rub them into felt
exercise: Why do sheep follow each other through a gate? | Staying together keeps them safer from predators | Sheep cannot see where they are going, so they just follow a tail | The first sheep is always their mother | Sheep forget where they are alone
exercise: What can sheep remember for years? | Dozens of faces of sheep and people | Only the face of their mother | Nothing, they forget in a minute | The colour of the gate
exercise: What does it mean when a sheep is cast? | It is stuck on its back and cannot get up | It has just been shorn | It has left the flock and gone off on its own to find food | It is having a lamb
exercise: How does a ewe find her own lamb in a crowded field? | By its call | By the number on its ear | By counting the lambs | She does not, any lamb will do
exercise: Why do lambs skip and play? | Play builds muscles and friendships | They are trying to scare foxes | They are too hot | They are looking for water
exercise: Why can a sheep graze grass very short? | It nibbles with a split top lip | It has no teeth at all, only gums | It pulls grass out by the roots with its feet | It has a longer tongue than a cow
exercise: Which animals chew the cud like cows do? | Sheep and goats | Horses and donkeys | Pigs and chickens | Dogs and farm cats
exercise: Why do goats love to climb? | Their wild relatives live on cliffs and steep mountains | They are looking for birds' nests | Their hooves get too hot on the ground in summer and need cool rocks | They are trying to get away from sheep
exercise: What do goats really prefer to eat? | Leaves, twigs and shrubs | Tin cans and nails | Only grass | Meat and eggs
exercise: Why does a goat nibble paper and labels? | It is curious and explores with its lips | It needs metal for its teeth | It cannot tell food from paper by smell, so it eats everything | It is always starving
exercise: What shape are a goat's pupils? | Wide and rectangular | Round, just like ours | Tall slits like a cat's | Shaped like little stars
exercise: How do a goat's pupils help it? | They give a wide view to spot predators | They let it see perfectly in total darkness | They help it read signs | They make it look friendly
exercise: How do goat mothers and kids recognise each other? | By their voices | By their long beards | By their ear tags | By the colour of their horns
exercise: Which goats can have beards? | Many goats, males and females | Only old male goats | Only goats in cartoons | No goats, only sheep
exercise: Why might a farmer keep a donkey with the sheep? | Donkeys chase away foxes and stray dogs | Donkeys eat the sheep's wool | Donkeys count the sheep | Donkeys keep the sheep warm at night by lying down in the middle of them
test: «my cousin says sheep are dead thick, they just follow anyone» What is right? | No, they follow the flock for safety and can remember dozens of faces | Yes, sheep cannot learn anything at all, not even their names | Yes, sheep are blind so they follow | No, sheep never follow each other
test: In a cartoon a goat crunches up a whole tin can and burps. What is real? | A goat may nibble the paper label, but it does not eat the tin | Goats eat tins on purpose to get the iron that their bones need | Goats eat tins only in winter | Goats can eat tins but not plastic
test: In Shaun the Sheep the sheep stand on two legs and talk. What is true of real sheep? | They walk on four legs and talk to each other with bleats | They walk on two legs at night when the farmer cannot see them | They have two legs and two arms | They only stand up to eat
test: «3 sheep, 2 goats n the donkey in the field. how many legs» | 24 | 20 | 18 | 22
test: «farm's got 4 goats and 5 sheep, how many ears then» | 18 | 9 | 36 | 16
test: «4 sheep 1 donkey, and a goat jumped on the car. how many tails, no trunks pls lol» | 6 | 5 | 4 | 0
test: «sheep on its back, legs waving, it's just chilling yeah» What should you do? | Tell the farmer at once, a stuck sheep can die | Leave it, sheep like to lie like that in the sunshine to warm up | Pour water on it to wake it | Roll it into the pond
test: What would happen if a farm sheep was never shorn for years? | Its wool would keep growing into a huge heavy fleece | Its wool would fall out by itself | It would turn into a goat | Its wool would stop at a short length
test: «why's that goat's eye got a letterbox in it» What is the answer? | Its wide rectangular pupil gives it a view almost all the way round | It has an eye illness | It is a cat's eye on a goat | It sees only straight ahead, like looking through a letterbox in a door
test: «how do u tell a goat from a sheep anyway» Which is a good clue? | A goat's tail usually points up, a sheep's hangs down | Goats have four legs and sheep have six | Sheep have beards and goats never do, even the old billy goats | Goats have no ears
test: A field of ewes and lambs is very noisy. Why are they all calling? | Mothers and lambs find each other by their calls | They are telling the farmer they are hungry and want more food | They are scaring away the shearer | Lambs are practising songs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the necessity of shearing domestic sheep? | Domestic breeds produce wool which grows continuously and is not shed | Wool must be removed lest the animal be mistaken for a goat | Shearing is required solely for the comfort of the shearer | Wild and domestic sheep alike require annual shearing
exam: Identify the principal reason for which sheep maintain close proximity to one another within a flock. | Aggregation affords protection from predators | Sheep are incapable of independent vision | The leading animal is invariably the mother of all | Proximity is required for the digestion of grass
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of the domestic goat. | Leaves, twigs and shrubs are preferred, and inedible objects are merely explored with the lips | Metallic containers are consumed and digested | Grass constitutes its exclusive diet | It subsists principally upon meat
exam: It is given that a sheep is found lying upon its back and unable to rise. Ascertain the appropriate course of action. | The farmer should be informed without delay, since such an animal may perish | The animal should be left undisturbed, as it is resting | The animal should be encouraged to remain in that position | Water should be poured upon the animal
